WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.635
[bear growls]

00:00:01.635 --> 00:00:02.677
Oh, s---.

00:00:05.968 --> 00:00:07.170
[screaming]

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.270
It scrambled down the tree,

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:11.580
and that type of speed is real.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.460
They can run up to 25 miles an hour.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.510
Hey, y'all. I'm Dr. Rae Wynn-Grant.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:17.850
I'm a wildlife ecologist,

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.610
and I've been studying bears for 13 years.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.592
Today we're going to be
looking at bear attacks

00:00:22.592 --> 00:00:24.000
in movies and on TV

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.533
and judging how real they are.

00:00:28.377 --> 00:00:29.827
[bear roars]

00:00:29.827 --> 00:00:31.200
So, already in this clip,

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.380
I see the very cinematic trope

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.720
of a giant brown bear

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.386
standing up on its hind legs
and roaring at a person.

00:00:40.386 --> 00:00:42.083
Honestly, in real life,
bears are very quiet.

00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:43.920
They really don't do a lot of growling.

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:45.240
They don't do a lot of roaring.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.490
But if there's a territory dispute,

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.340
two big male alpha bears

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.440
might actually stand up on their hind legs

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:54.180
and roar at each other and fight.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:57.360
But if there's a big
bear and a small person,

00:00:57.360 --> 00:00:58.468
that bear isn't usually going to

00:00:58.468 --> 00:00:59.518
go through all that trouble.

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.920
You saw that big swipe of the ground.

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.553
That's something that brown
bears and black bears do.

00:01:11.553 --> 00:01:13.740
In fact, I have experienced

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:15.840
a bear do a bluff charge on me,

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.090
and it is frightening and terrifying.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.730
But really, they're just
trying to freak you out

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.950
so that you back up and walk away

00:01:22.950 --> 00:01:24.741
and they don't actually
have to attack you.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.840
This is great for TV, but it's
not biologically accurate.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.140
This bear, even though it looks like

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:40.050
it could be 1,000 pounds,

00:01:40.050 --> 00:01:42.210
it could scale that wall no problem.

00:01:42.210 --> 00:01:43.980
Those claws could dig into the wood,

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.080
and it could climb up all the way

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.455
and then terrorize all those
people that are watching.

00:01:48.455 --> 00:01:50.430
I'd give it a seven out of 10.

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:53.105
I really wish the bear
had climbed up at the end.

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:00.480
Let me make it clear.

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:02.880
That is what you don't do.

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:05.640
Even if it looks like you
might be attacked by a bear,

00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:06.870
you never run.

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.450
It's because humans cannot outrun bears.

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:10.920
When humans run,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.140
it signals to the
predator that you're prey.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:14.820
Like, that's what a little deer would do

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.110
or a little rabbit would do.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:17.670
But when we stand still,

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.500
that's so different
than what prey would do

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.225
that they get the message.

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:27.420
No way!

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:29.430
Bears, as big and burly as they are,

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.300
can also get into teeny,
tiny, small spaces.

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.190
I mean, I've seen bears

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:37.590
squeeze themselves into
anything they want.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.857
So, again, if it wanted
to hurt him, it would.

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:44.873
This is interesting.

00:02:44.873 --> 00:02:47.550
So, we see that they made a ring of fire,

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.640
and that's their tactic
of protecting themselves.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:54.648
First, no one should ever do
this in the wilderness. OK?

00:02:54.648 --> 00:02:56.520
It will start a forest fire.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.040
But, two, there really
is something to this,

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:01.770
and that's that most wild
animals are afraid of fire.

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:03.845
It's just something that they
haven't experienced, right?

00:03:03.845 --> 00:03:05.340
They can't make fire themselves.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:10.232
So as much as I don't want
anyone to ever use this tactic,

00:03:10.232 --> 00:03:12.810
I believe it would be
effective to keep you safe.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.187
How are we going to lure him?

00:03:17.342 --> 00:03:18.210
Blood!

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:20.135
Did you see him? "Blood!"

00:03:20.135 --> 00:03:21.982
That's not going to work. OK?

00:03:21.982 --> 00:03:26.104
So, bears are driven by hunger.

00:03:26.104 --> 00:03:28.290
Blood does not necessarily signal

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.790
this is something to eat, right?

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.100
A bear wants to smell something delicious.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:34.110
It wants to smell honey.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.450
It wants to smell rotting meat.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.340
I think they're getting
this from the shark thing,

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:39.930
like, sharks are drawn to blood.

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:41.222
It's not the same with bears.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.890
So, this is actually something
that we use in real life.

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.620
If we want to lure a bear to something,

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:51.660
maybe we set up a little trail cam

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:54.693
and we want to get information
on the individual bear,

00:03:54.693 --> 00:03:56.800
we'll put out what we call a scent rag.

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:57.930
And so it looks just like that,

00:03:57.930 --> 00:03:59.267
like a little piece of cloth,

00:03:59.267 --> 00:04:01.110
and we'll put some kind of scent on it.

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:03.960
We could literally use,
like, vanilla extract.

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:07.135
They'll come right to that
rag to see what it is.

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.170
Is it realistic that that bear

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:20.640
might come looking for you again

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.800
because you are still in its territory

00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:24.660
and haven't left? Yes.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.400
However, is it realistic

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:28.770
that a bear is going
to attack over and over

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:32.079
when there's two or more people? No.

00:04:32.079 --> 00:04:33.540
So, bear attacks happen usually

00:04:33.540 --> 00:04:35.190
when there's just one
person by themselves.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.865
If you have two or
more, it rarely happens,

00:04:37.865 --> 00:04:39.994
which is another way
that people can be safe

00:04:39.994 --> 00:04:41.340
when they recreate in nature.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:42.757
It did look real,

00:04:42.757 --> 00:04:46.050
but the probability of something like this

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:47.760
actually taking place, like,

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:49.785
I'm going to give this a five out of 10.

00:04:54.150 --> 00:04:57.034
The first shot we see of the
bear is of its claws, right?

00:04:57.034 --> 00:04:58.590
Those are real claws.

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:00.330
They did a great job modeling that.

00:05:00.330 --> 00:05:01.650
It looks like a real animal.

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.530
And then the other thing we see
is the bear sniffing, right?

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:05.363
Meh.

00:05:05.363 --> 00:05:08.760
Bears' sense of smell is so precise

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.890
and can go across such a long distance

00:05:10.890 --> 00:05:12.990
that they're not going to be
sniffing around for a person

00:05:12.990 --> 00:05:14.970
when they're literally 2 feet from them.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:16.560
Bears can't climb trees.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:18.360
Of course they can!

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:20.522
This little kid just said,

00:05:20.522 --> 00:05:22.785
"We're safe, 'cause bears
can't climb trees," right?

00:05:22.785 --> 00:05:25.170
Already I'm like, "Oh, no!"

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:26.730
Like, they're doomed.

00:05:26.730 --> 00:05:28.563
Bears are the best tree climbers.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.190
Henry, move!

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:33.023
Go higher!

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:40.140
He's kicking downward with his leg.

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.691
If you ever find yourself above a bear

00:05:43.691 --> 00:05:45.292
on a tree, you should do that.

00:05:45.292 --> 00:05:47.808
But climbing higher? No, no, no, no, no.

00:05:47.808 --> 00:05:49.020
There's a phrase out there,

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:50.550
when it comes to bear attacks,

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.870
"If it's black, fight back;

00:05:51.870 --> 00:05:53.280
if it's brown, lay down."

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:54.720
And it's important to note

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:56.670
that this is for bear attacks.

00:05:56.670 --> 00:05:59.370
This is not for bear encounters.

00:05:59.370 --> 00:06:01.110
Even if a bear is near you,

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:03.690
even if it's approaching you rapidly,

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:05.040
that's still not an attack

00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:08.100
until it's actually
physically touching you.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:11.190
That is a really important difference,

00:06:11.190 --> 00:06:13.140
because if a bear is not attacking you,

00:06:13.140 --> 00:06:15.270
that's when you back away slowly,

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:16.890
you make yourself look big.

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:19.715
If it is attacking you,
the kid did it right.

00:06:19.715 --> 00:06:22.731
But the whole time, also
try to get out of there.

00:06:24.985 --> 00:06:26.052
Oh, s---.

00:06:31.271 --> 00:06:34.171
Ah! It scrambled down the tree,

00:06:34.171 --> 00:06:36.600
and that type of speed is real.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:40.440
So, the bear first slowly
went up to get the boy.

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.670
Of course it can do that.

00:06:41.670 --> 00:06:44.712
But the way it practically
leapt onto the second tree

00:06:44.712 --> 00:06:46.080
and scaled it in a second,

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.090
that's really, when a
bear wants something,

00:06:48.090 --> 00:06:51.240
I mean, they can run
up to 25 miles an hour.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:55.298
Also, it should be said
that bears don't do drugs.

00:06:55.298 --> 00:06:59.358
It is totally hypothetical
what a bear would do

00:06:59.358 --> 00:07:03.690
if it was on tons and tons of cocaine.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:07.183
But this movie is loosely
based off of a true story.

00:07:07.183 --> 00:07:08.910
But there was a bear in the '80s,

00:07:08.910 --> 00:07:10.590
I believe it was 1985,

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:12.540
that was found dead

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.560
after ingesting a big amount of cocaine

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:18.720
that was dropped from an airplane.

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:22.023
But in general, overdoses can kill animals

00:07:22.023 --> 00:07:23.370
just like they can kill people,

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:26.362
and that's what happened
to the real cocaine beer.

00:07:30.930 --> 00:07:34.230
The only thing that I saw in this clip

00:07:34.230 --> 00:07:36.580
that wasn't so accurate
is that they're really

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.850
not that interested in
attacking people, right?

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:40.230
Especially black bears.

00:07:40.230 --> 00:07:42.660
Black bears are the least predatory

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:44.340
of all the bear species.

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:47.250
They mostly have a vegetarian diet.

00:07:47.250 --> 00:07:49.141
They love their fruit,
they love their nuts.

00:07:49.141 --> 00:07:50.580
Another thing people should know

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:53.250
is that bears do not eat people.

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:55.020
Sometimes it's happened,

00:07:55.020 --> 00:07:57.180
like, really, really rarely,

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:00.150
but they do not prey on humans.

00:08:00.150 --> 00:08:02.100
If a bear is attacking someone,

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:04.925
it's really because they
perceive that person as a threat.

00:08:04.925 --> 00:08:07.380
I would give it, like, a six out of 10.

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:12.180
I can't imagine a bear
ever leaving one person

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:15.213
to go attack another person for drugs.

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.724
[bear groans]

00:08:19.724 --> 00:08:22.391
[bear growling]

00:08:24.238 --> 00:08:26.220
Oh, oh, oh! That's real.

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:30.245
Right behind him comes
what's gotta be mama bear.

00:08:30.245 --> 00:08:33.000
The female bear raises her cubs,

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:36.873
and she dedicates her
life to keeping them safe.

00:08:36.873 --> 00:08:39.270
This guy was technically
just standing there.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:44.220
But because he was between
the bear and her cub,

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:47.412
she sees that as a threat,
and she ran right through him.

00:08:47.412 --> 00:08:48.960
So, we often tell people

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.360
who are recreating in the wilderness,

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:54.427
do not physically come between
a female bear and her cub.

00:08:54.427 --> 00:08:57.000
Even if you notice a bear cub on its own

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.740
and you do not see the mama,

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:01.620
get away from there immediately.

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.270
You are in danger. OK?

00:09:03.270 --> 00:09:06.670
They lose their mind,
and they just attack.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:09.420
[Hugh screaming]

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:15.450
So, it's actually really, really accurate

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:17.190
the way that they showed the attack.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.400
The way that the bear
uses her teeth the most --

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.010
like, she does a little
bit of paw stuff --

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:25.800
she uses those parts of her the most

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:27.510
because they're the most effective.

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:30.135
And, again, she's not trying
to get a bite to eat, right?

00:09:30.135 --> 00:09:31.200
If she wanted to eat him,

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.840
she would use her claws to tear him apart.

00:09:33.840 --> 00:09:35.100
But instead, she just wants to have

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.620
those fatal bites to keep her cubs safe.

00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:40.830
But the biggest myth that
the media shows about bears

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:43.380
is that they're always on the attack.

00:09:43.380 --> 00:09:45.060
In some of the national
parks that have bears,

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.560
take Yellowstone National Park,

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:48.480
your chances of a bear attack

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:51.420
are something like one in 2 million.

00:09:51.420 --> 00:09:54.695
So, truly, bears are
peaceful. They're docile.

00:09:54.695 --> 00:09:57.398
And with that said, don't bother them,

00:09:57.398 --> 00:10:00.307
don't frustrate them,
and don't trigger them,

00:10:00.307 --> 00:10:02.758
because if you do, you
might be in trouble.

00:10:06.115 --> 00:10:07.656
[Hugh grunting]

00:10:07.656 --> 00:10:10.500
Like, at this exact spot,

00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:14.100
I wish the character knew to play dead.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.510
Because this is a brown bear,

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.070
sometimes called grizzly bears,

00:10:17.070 --> 00:10:20.419
and with that species it's
really important to play dead.

00:10:20.419 --> 00:10:23.730
'Cause if he just played dead right there,

00:10:23.730 --> 00:10:25.290
it's more likely that she would, like,

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:27.757
nudge him a little bit
more just to make sure

00:10:27.757 --> 00:10:29.670
and then walk away.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:31.020
But he's, like,

00:10:31.020 --> 00:10:33.120
still making a little bit of noise,

00:10:33.120 --> 00:10:35.353
and it's telling her,
"Nope, he's still alive.

00:10:35.353 --> 00:10:36.548
He could still be a threat."

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.080
This is giving me anxiety.

00:10:43.080 --> 00:10:45.605
He shot her and then
she just attacked more,

00:10:45.605 --> 00:10:47.283
'cause she's like, "See, I was right.

00:10:47.283 --> 00:10:48.210
It just tried to hurt me.

00:10:48.210 --> 00:10:51.585
I'm just going to kill it
and rip it to pieces now."

00:10:51.585 --> 00:10:53.910
So I can understand having a weapon

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:57.693
and wanting to kill it and
fight back for your life.

00:10:57.693 --> 00:11:01.410
But, again, you're safer playing dead

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:05.010
until he can no longer hear
or see the bear or her cub.

00:11:05.010 --> 00:11:07.230
Honestly, I'd give this
one a 10 out of 10.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.697
It's, believe it or not, a CGI bear,

00:11:09.697 --> 00:11:10.832
but they really got it right.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:13.922
How did you get up there?

00:11:13.922 --> 00:11:14.760
I don't know.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.444
Of course pandas don't
really do the splits,

00:11:16.444 --> 00:11:17.940
but they are flexible.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:19.920
All the little YouTube videos you see

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.690
of pandas in captivity

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.460
rolling around and being cute,

00:11:23.460 --> 00:11:25.980
they really do have very flexible bodies

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.748
although they're chubby and fat.

00:11:27.748 --> 00:11:30.988
I now see that the way
to get through to you

00:11:30.988 --> 00:11:32.176
is with this.

00:11:32.176 --> 00:11:33.390
Oh, great.

00:11:33.390 --> 00:11:36.960
Realistic that they would
want to eat a lot of food,

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:39.660
but would they eat
delicious Chinese pork buns?

00:11:39.660 --> 00:11:41.550
I don't think so.

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:45.960
However, a lot of people
don't realize that pandas,

00:11:45.960 --> 00:11:47.670
although they're vegetarians

00:11:47.670 --> 00:11:50.070
and some might think they're herbivores,

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:52.470
they're actually still carnivores.

00:11:52.470 --> 00:11:56.850
Pandas have the gut
microbiome to digest meat,

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:00.575
meaning they have the bacterial
community in their bodies

00:12:00.575 --> 00:12:04.203
to break down meat and
synthesize it for energy.

00:12:04.203 --> 00:12:05.880
So that means that it's possible

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:08.220
that ancient pandas were carnivores

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:09.840
and ate meat all the time,

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:12.365
and probably if they
wanted to they still could.

00:12:20.070 --> 00:12:23.253
So, you know, this panda
is learning martial arts,

00:12:23.253 --> 00:12:26.910
which is atypical for wild pandas,

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.051
but believe it or not, pandas can fight.

00:12:29.051 --> 00:12:31.807
Pandas have claws and they have fangs,

00:12:31.807 --> 00:12:36.242
long fangs that could rip
through tissue really easily.

00:12:36.242 --> 00:12:39.870
So they have the physical structure

00:12:39.870 --> 00:12:43.170
to be very, very aggressive
and violent if they want to.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.400
I love this movie,

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:48.574
but I have to give it a one out of 10.

00:12:48.574 --> 00:12:51.241
[bears roaring]

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:56.760
I'm always happy to see polar bears.

00:12:56.760 --> 00:12:58.110
They're awesome.

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:00.360
They are not only the largest

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:01.920
of all eight bear species,

00:13:01.920 --> 00:13:04.650
they are also the largest
carnivore on the planet,

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:06.150
and they are very aggressive.

00:13:06.150 --> 00:13:10.080
So to have a very violent interaction

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:13.663
between two alpha male
polar bears is realistic.

00:13:13.663 --> 00:13:14.760
They're not going to have, like,

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:17.160
an audience of other polar bears.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:19.770
Maybe if there's a big common food source,

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:21.210
they would congregate together.

00:13:21.210 --> 00:13:23.668
Other than that, they're not
really cooperating like that.

00:13:31.950 --> 00:13:34.200
The other thing that
we wouldn't really see

00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:36.240
in a polar bear-polar bear fight

00:13:36.240 --> 00:13:39.300
is the running and slamming together.

00:13:39.300 --> 00:13:40.530
Usually when they're fighting

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:42.420
they approach each other slowly,

00:13:42.420 --> 00:13:44.520
they'll do all the bluff
charges to each other,

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:46.110
and then they'll get up on their hind legs

00:13:46.110 --> 00:13:47.190
and start fighting.

00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:49.700
But they won't kind of
knock each other back

00:13:49.700 --> 00:13:51.886
and then run at each other together again.

00:13:55.170 --> 00:13:57.511
They don't eat people,
they don't eat each other,

00:13:57.511 --> 00:13:59.985
but they're more likely to
be extremely aggressive.

00:13:59.985 --> 00:14:02.886
Polar bears are what we call
obligate carnivores, right?

00:14:02.886 --> 00:14:05.670
So that means they just want
to hunt and kill and eat,

00:14:05.670 --> 00:14:07.870
and because of that it
impacts their behavior.

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:09.810
They often say, like,

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:11.820
"If it's white, goodnight."

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:15.630
It means that if you are in
an attack with a polar bear,

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:16.710
it's not looking good.

00:14:16.710 --> 00:14:19.410
As well done as the art was,

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.703
I would give it, like, a two out of 10.

00:14:21.703 --> 00:14:22.786
Bear fight!

00:14:26.070 --> 00:14:27.360
I think it goes without saying

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:30.450
that this is extremely exaggerated.

00:14:30.450 --> 00:14:32.700
The bear was, like, slapping back,

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:34.650
and that was actually kind of realistic.

00:14:34.650 --> 00:14:36.691
In fact, it doesn't take
a lot of energy for them

00:14:36.691 --> 00:14:38.160
to slap you across the face.

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:39.570
But punching a bear in the face

00:14:39.570 --> 00:14:42.090
is just a really great
way to irritate them

00:14:42.090 --> 00:14:44.495
and to get them to really hurt you.

00:14:44.495 --> 00:14:47.490
Hey, Ron, I'm riding a furry tractor!

00:14:47.490 --> 00:14:48.720
You can't ride a bear.

00:14:48.720 --> 00:14:52.470
It's kind of worth explaining
that over the years

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:56.040
bears have been used in circuses

00:14:56.040 --> 00:14:58.380
and entertainment capacity.

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:00.390
Don't train wild animals.

00:15:00.390 --> 00:15:02.700
There's a lot of ethics around this.

00:15:02.700 --> 00:15:05.610
Probably bear riding has been a thing.

00:15:05.610 --> 00:15:06.910
But these days,

00:15:06.910 --> 00:15:08.435
you're not really going
to find it anywhere.

00:15:08.435 --> 00:15:09.540
I would give it, again,

00:15:09.540 --> 00:15:11.763
like, a three out of 10.

00:15:13.687 --> 00:15:15.604
[bear growling]
Oh, f---.

00:15:16.656 --> 00:15:18.150
Oh! Ah!

00:15:18.150 --> 00:15:20.360
That's something that every
camper is worried about.

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:23.052
If they're camping in a
place where bears could be,

00:15:23.052 --> 00:15:26.478
bears will smell something
good in your tent

00:15:26.478 --> 00:15:28.080
and will come looking for it.

00:15:28.080 --> 00:15:31.680
So, it looks like she just
uncapped her bear spray.

00:15:31.680 --> 00:15:32.700
Really glad to see that.

00:15:32.700 --> 00:15:34.680
Bear spray is something that I have on me

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:37.110
at all times whenever
I'm in the wilderness.

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:38.210
It can save your life.

00:15:45.045 --> 00:15:46.350
Ooh!

00:15:46.350 --> 00:15:48.420
This is where this woman went wrong.

00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:52.170
Bear spray is completely, 100% ineffective

00:15:52.170 --> 00:15:54.240
when you're in close
proximity with the bear.

00:15:54.240 --> 00:15:56.380
It's just going to aggravate
it and make it attack you.

00:15:56.380 --> 00:15:58.620
You use it when there's
a bear in the distance.

00:15:58.620 --> 00:16:01.200
Bear spray is pepper spray,

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:02.580
but it's not the same kind of pepper spray

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:05.070
that we use to keep ourselves safe.

00:16:05.070 --> 00:16:07.560
It is extremely powerful,

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.990
and it shoots at a long distance.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:12.900
So if you sprayed bear spray in your tent,

00:16:12.900 --> 00:16:14.850
you would not be able to breathe.

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:16.342
You wouldn't be able to see.

00:16:16.342 --> 00:16:18.030
You would be incapacitated.

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:20.910
You have to do it in the open,

00:16:20.910 --> 00:16:23.100
and then you have to get
yourself out of there.

00:16:23.100 --> 00:16:24.455
So what they should have done is,

00:16:24.455 --> 00:16:27.397
at the beginning of the
clip, unzip the tent,

00:16:27.397 --> 00:16:30.603
spray the bear spray, zip it back up,

00:16:30.603 --> 00:16:32.467
and wait a few minutes
and see what happens.

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:37.920
That one was intense.

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:42.128
It would have to have so
many interactions with humans

00:16:42.128 --> 00:16:46.595
that it learns a behavior
to become a killer,

00:16:46.595 --> 00:16:50.520
like a deliberate predator of humans.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:53.612
Also, the bear-spray situation
could have saved them

00:16:53.612 --> 00:16:56.160
way before the attack actually happened.

00:16:56.160 --> 00:16:58.980
I wish they had just deployed
the bear spray the right way.

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:01.410
I wanted to give it,
like, a nine out of 10,

00:17:01.410 --> 00:17:04.560
'cause I was like,
camping, black bear, fear,

00:17:04.560 --> 00:17:05.640
like, that all makes sense.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:08.250
But then in terms of how aggressive

00:17:08.250 --> 00:17:10.890
and how murderous that bear was,

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:13.073
I want to give it, like, a four out of 10.

00:17:20.820 --> 00:17:22.410
Oh, my God, what is that?

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:25.752
Kind of has the humps
and lumps of a bear, ish.

00:17:25.752 --> 00:17:28.385
We can see it has this
extended kind of snout,

00:17:28.385 --> 00:17:31.613
almost like a horse head,
and then a more flat head.

00:17:31.613 --> 00:17:33.540
Black bear will either
have the extended snout

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:35.250
but then a proper hump for head,

00:17:35.250 --> 00:17:37.170
or a brown bear or a grizzly bear

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:41.345
has more of the flattened
face and a much rounder head.

00:17:41.345 --> 00:17:42.873
It's all over the place.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.120
One of the things that
I'm also noticing is that,

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:50.353
looks like there's places
where it's missing fur

00:17:50.353 --> 00:17:52.440
and just has raw skin.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:55.081
And believe it or not, this
is actually kind of accurate,

00:17:55.081 --> 00:17:57.180
because there are times

00:17:57.180 --> 00:17:59.970
when bears can be very
susceptible to mange,

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.070
the same mange that dogs can get.

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:05.460
So it's possible that a mangy bear

00:18:05.460 --> 00:18:09.150
influenced this animal here.

00:18:09.150 --> 00:18:11.400
OK, I'm going to give it a one out of 10.

00:18:11.400 --> 00:18:13.110
Like, that wasn't even a real animal.

00:18:13.110 --> 00:18:15.810
My favorite bear show

00:18:15.810 --> 00:18:18.060
is a show that I've actually been in,

00:18:18.060 --> 00:18:19.230
and I don't think it's fair

00:18:19.230 --> 00:18:21.450
to really toot my own horn right here,

00:18:21.450 --> 00:18:25.590
so I will say that my
second-favorite bear show

00:18:25.590 --> 00:18:27.330
was "Yogi Bear."

00:18:27.330 --> 00:18:29.520
And it was mostly about
this bear named Yogi

00:18:29.520 --> 00:18:30.990
who lived in a national park

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:33.270
and would steal people's picnic baskets.

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:36.330
But it's kind of realistic, right?

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.572
Like, bears are in a lot of
America's national parks,

00:18:39.572 --> 00:18:41.670
and they love our food.

00:18:41.670 --> 00:18:43.680
That's something that humans
and bears have in common.

00:18:43.680 --> 00:18:44.880
Thanks for watching.

00:18:44.880 --> 00:18:48.922
If you liked this video, why
not click on the video above?

